      "id": "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b4403c0d-d960-5109-a9d0-df0f5fc2545a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124473/plots/plot-f06576d0f77ab95a4b1c0fb139ec6b78f70253619fac46c53f5de47f69f72da3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in [-1,3]$\n\nII. $x\\in (-4,-1]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-2,3]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in [-1,3]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in (-4,-1]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-2,3]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ff38d644-68fc-55f1-b493-35dee0eeda55",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
